\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces Memory degradation does not act uniformly. (a)~Graded truncation: no detectable increase in unsupported completion; the Wilson 95\% intervals mark this a non-detection at modest power, not invariance. (b)~Replacing history with a resume summary does produce the effect: the evidence-citation prompt reduces unsupported proposals ($5{\to }3$), and the gate converts those it sees from silent acceptances into blocked episodes that recover (2 of 2). Small $n$; decomposition, not significance.}}{3}{figure.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:regimes}{{2}{3}{Memory degradation does not act uniformly. (a)~Graded truncation: no detectable increase in unsupported completion; the Wilson 95\% intervals mark this a non-detection at modest power, not invariance. (b)~Replacing history with a resume summary does produce the effect: the evidence-citation prompt reduces unsupported proposals ($5{\to }3$), and the gate converts those it sees from silent acceptances into blocked episodes that recover (2 of 2). Small $n$; decomposition, not significance}{figure.2}{}}
